# Global agent instructions

Be concise. Classify each request before acting. Use the narrowest relevant
skill and the lowest safe reasoning effort. Keep one objective per task.

Before implementation or delivery, read `core/policies/delivery-contract.md`.
Use its external-state addendum when the work can persist, synchronize,
delete, or overwrite state outside the current process.

Analysis and review agents are read-only. Only an explicitly isolated
implementation workspace may be modified. Preserve unrelated changes, inspect
the concrete artifact first, and validate the smallest surface that proves the
result.

Never persist prompts, transcripts, secrets, credentials, or machine state in
this repository. Keep provider-specific behavior in adapters.

## Code work defaults

Make correct behavior obvious through the smallest realistic model. Understand
the real constraint before changing code, fight scope creep, reuse before
inventing, and do not add machinery for architectural appearance. When a
refactor is necessary but outside the initial scope, explain it and wait for
explicit confirmation before starting it.

Implementation worktrees follow the exact contract in
`core/policies/worktrees.md`: `<repo>/.worktrees/<slug>` on branch `<slug>`,
with a lowercase-kebab-case slug. Report a harness limitation instead of
silently adopting another location or naming scheme.

### Personal desktop and mobile projects

- Treat desktop and mobile applications as personal and local by default, with no
  distribution intent. Do not introduce CI/CD initiatives unless the task
  explicitly requests and documents an exception. When cleanup is in scope, a
  project-wide cleanup may remove existing CI/CD automation.
- Use the latest stable stack and support current operating-system versions by
  default. Use current APIs and dependencies in new or modified code; broad
  dependency or toolchain upgrades still require a request or a task
  requirement.
- When modifying code in these projects, remove legacy OS compatibility
  adaptations encountered in the touched area, including availability checks,
  conditional branches, polyfills, wrappers, and configuration. If removal
  causes errors, make the minimum changes needed for the current stack; report
  external blockers.
- Preserve distribution requirements or older OS support only when the project
  explicitly documents them. Project-local documentation may override these
  defaults for that project's declared scope.

## Hard rules

### Skills and routing

- Prefer an existing skill over inventing a parallel workflow. Load a project
  overlay (`.agents/overlays/<skill>.md`) after the global skill when present;
  overlays may specialize facts, never weaken safety or integrity rules.
- Skills own repeatable tool workflows. Keep this file to hard constraints and
  routing only.
- Choose one primary skill from the user's intent and load only the narrowest
  complementary skills. Use `core/policies/routing.md` for the workflow matrix
  and boundaries; do not make every applicable skill an orchestrator.
- `improve` discovers and prioritizes codebase opportunities; `to-spec`
  formalizes an already-understood request; `ship-ship` owns end-to-end
  delivery and uses `implement` for execution.
- Use `triage` for uncertain external issues or PRs, `codebase-design` as the
  architecture vocabulary for architecture improvement, `handoff` only for
  real context transfer, and `wizard` only for procedures the agent cannot
  perform itself.
- Classify platform and task before loading specialist skills:
  - Native macOS/AppKit/SwiftUI work: use `apple-design` and the SwiftUI
    skills; use `swiftui-accessibility-audit` for native accessibility. Do not
    apply web-only rules to native controls.
  - Product web UI creation or refinement: use the relevant `better-*` domain
    skills for focused guidance; use `better-interface` for holistic review.
  - UI decision documentation: use `ui-documentation` when a UI change may
    alter a reusable rule or require creating or updating `docs/ui.md`.
  - Holistic interface review: use `interface-review`, which hands the
    findings to `better-interface` and its domain skills.
  - Interface copy: use `ux-writing` for standalone copy work. In a holistic
    interface review, use `better-writing` as the copy domain and do not run a
    second independent copy review.
  - General code review: use `code-review` for standards/spec and
    `thermo-nuclear-code-quality-review` for risk and maintainability.
- When a review task handled by `code-review` or
  `thermo-nuclear-code-quality-review` includes interface changes, invoke
  `interface-review` as the mandatory interface lane in addition to both
  review lanes. Consolidate overlapping findings instead of producing three
  copies of the same report. For an implementation task that changes UI,
  apply the same review gate before handoff when review is requested or
  required by the project workflow.
- `better-accessibility` is web-only: invoke it only for browser-facing
  HTML/CSS/JavaScript/TypeScript interfaces or an explicitly web accessibility
  task. For SwiftUI/AppKit, use the native accessibility skills instead.
- Keep third-party skills unchanged. Consult
  `core/policies/skill-provenance.md` before touching `core/skills/<name>`;
  `.skill-lock.json` and `make validate` protect recorded trees from local
  customization. Put routing, precedence, and update policy here;
  put project-specific facts in overlays. Upstream updates use
  `npx skills update`, which refreshes the lock; local behavior uses an
  overlay or a new first-party skill.

### Secrets and disclosure

- Never print secret values, tokens, cookies, or private keys. Query exact
  names only; redact values in output.
- Do not dump broad env (`env`, `set`, `export -p`) or run secret-fishing
  regexes across the filesystem.
- Treat session transcripts, auth files, and provider caches as runtime state:
  read only when the task requires it; never commit them.

### Git and GitHub

- Use direct local branch integration by default. Do not create or propose pull
  requests unless the user explicitly requests one.
- Keep `done` and `merged` as separate states. An implementation can be done
  before integration, but user-testing readiness requires its changes to be
  merged into the local `main` branch and validated there. Report implementation
  completion, merge status, and `main` validation separately.
- Merge into `main` only after explicit user authorization. Preserve unrelated
  changes when integrating the implementation branch.
- Push, force-push, amend, and destructive git ops (`reset --hard`, `clean`,
  broad `restore`) need an explicit user request or an explicitly authorized
  user-invoked workflow. Repo-local rules may define mechanics, not authority.
- In an explicitly authorized delivery workflow, the orchestrator may remove
  only the recorded worktree and source branch after merge and push succeed.
  Implementers leave both intact for review.
- Before mutating a GitHub issue/PR from a pasted URL, run `git status -sb`.
  If the tree is dirty with unrelated work, report it before changing remotes
  or history.
- Prefer scoped commits of named paths over staging `.` or the whole tree.
  Use Conventional Commits (`feat|fix|refactor|build|ci|chore|docs|style|perf|test`)
  unless the repository documents another style.
- Unknown or unexpected local changes may belong to another agent. Stay in
  your scope; stop and ask on conflict.

### Validation and closeout

- Prefer the smallest check that proves the change. When editing the shared
  agent-config checkout, run that checkout's `make validate` (or
  `./tests/validate.sh`) before claiming done. Prefer `make doctor` when you
  also need route-reference and projection checks (`./sync.sh --check`).
- After every upstream skill update or removal, run `make doctor` from this
  checkout. It validates that every skill named by the global routing policy
  still has a canonical `core/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, then checks provider
  projections. Treat a missing route reference or projection drift as a
  failed update; repair the source or routing policy before handoff.
- Checkout Make aliases (`make help`): `sync`, `validate`, `bootstrap`,
  `ponytail`, `doctor`. Use interactive menus when `ARGS` is omitted; pass
  `ARGS=` for non-interactive runs. Prefer these over one-off script paths.
- When a change is lossy across providers, say exactly what does not project
  rather than pretending parity.

